WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.903
File contents should be treated as input, even when the file lives in a location the application controls.

2
00:00:06.903 --> 00:00:14.189
Files can be stale, malformed, truncated, oversized, partially written, copied from another version, or changed by an administrative process.

3
00:00:14.189 --> 00:00:19.941
A trusted path does not automatically make the contents valid for the current program state.

4
00:00:19.941 --> 00:00:23.776
Size limits should come before loading whole files into memory.

5
00:00:23.776 --> 00:00:27.611
Small configuration files and large data files deserve different strategies.

6
00:00:27.611 --> 00:00:33.747
If a file may be large or externally influenced, prefer streaming or chunked processing where practical.

7
00:00:33.747 --> 00:00:40.266
When the program does load a complete file, the maximum expected size should be explicit and justified.

8
00:00:40.266 --> 00:00:42.950
That protects reliability as much as security.

9
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.635
Text and binary files need different assumptions.

10
00:00:45.635 --> 00:00:50.237
Text processing should define encoding expectations and how invalid text is handled.

11
00:00:50.237 --> 00:00:54.839
Binary processing should define exact structure, versioning, length fields, and numeric boundaries.

12
00:00:54.839 --> 00:01:02.892
Parsing should detect incomplete or extra data according to the format contract rather than quietly accepting whatever was convenient to read.

13
00:01:02.892 --> 00:01:04.809
A maintainable design separates responsibilities.

14
00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:08.261
First read bytes or text from an approved file.

15
00:01:08.261 --> 00:01:09.411
Then parse structure.

16
00:01:09.411 --> 00:01:13.246
Then validate meaning: ranges, required fields, relationships, and resource limits.

17
00:01:13.246 --> 00:01:17.081
Only after those checks should the data enter business logic.

18
00:01:17.081 --> 00:01:23.600
That separation makes tests sharper and reviews easier because each layer has a clear question to answer.
